WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.296
You arrive at a hotel in another country, with your phone charger from home.

2
00:00:07.296 --> 00:00:09.897
The socket in the wall has the wrong shape.

3
00:00:09.897 --> 00:00:12.436
So you push a travel plug adapter into it.

4
00:00:12.436 --> 00:00:14.009
The socket stays the same.

5
00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:15.642
The charger stays the same.

6
00:00:15.642 --> 00:00:18.484
And the little block in between makes them fit.

7
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.832
The corner shop has the same problem.

8
00:00:21.832 --> 00:00:28.668
It books its deliveries through a courier company's old client library, a program the shop can't change.

9
00:00:28.668 --> 00:00:32.283
It speaks XML, a text format of tags in angle brackets.

10
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:36.621
And when the courier's office is closed, it throws an IOException.

11
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:42.771
IOException is a checked exception: one the Java compiler makes you catch, or declare.

12
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.955
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

13
00:00:46.955 --> 00:00:50.741
Why not just call the old library, everywhere we need it?

14
00:00:50.741 --> 00:00:51.339
It works.

15
00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:53.199
Why put anything in between?

16
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:55.872
Let's see where that leads.

17
00:00:55.872 --> 00:00:58.957
The website builds XML and catches IOException.

18
00:00:58.957 --> 00:01:00.467
So does the phone line.

19
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:02.108
So does the shop counter.

20
00:01:02.108 --> 00:01:04.667
Each in its own slightly different way.

21
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:09.393
And the day the shop switches couriers, every one of them has to change.

22
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.276
The old courier's words have moved in, and they've brought furniture.

23
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:20.248
What we'd like is for the shop's code to speak only the shop's own words, and for one place to do the translating.

24
00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:23.790
So the shop writes down its own words first.

25
00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:31.242
An interface, Courier, with one method: book a parcel for an address and a weight in grams, and get back a booking reference.

26
00:01:31.242 --> 00:01:37.144
An interface like that, in your own words, that the rest of your code depends on, is called a port.

27
00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:39.616
Now map it to the hotel.

28
00:01:39.616 --> 00:01:43.619
The socket in the wall is the old client: you can't change it.

29
00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:45.685
Your charger is the shop's code.

30
00:01:45.685 --> 00:01:47.880
The shape of its plug is the port.

31
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.689
And the block in between is the adapter: OldCourierAdapter.

32
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.708
Let's follow one booking through the adapter.

33
00:01:55.708 --> 00:01:57.434
12 Mill Lane, 2500 grams.

34
00:01:57.434 --> 00:02:03.649
First, it writes the request in their format: a parcel tag with the grams and the address.

35
00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:05.513
Then it calls them.

36
00:02:05.513 --> 00:02:10.451
The old client answers in its own format: ok, ref R-2500.

37
00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:15.256
Then the adapter cuts our booking reference out of their answer.

38
00:02:15.256 --> 00:02:15.758
R-2500.

39
00:02:15.758 --> 00:02:17.407
Their format ends here.

40
00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:21.878
Now try an address the courier can't reach: 1 Nowhere Road.

41
00:02:21.878 --> 00:02:24.354
The old client throws its IOException.

42
00:02:24.354 --> 00:02:26.309
What does the shop's code see?

43
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.990
It sees the shop's own exception, CourierUnavailable: courier unreachable.

44
00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:38.807
The catch in the adapter stopped their exception, and put ours in its place.

45
00:02:39.733 --> 00:02:43.298
Here's what the lesson's XmlAdapterDemo printed.

46
00:02:43.298 --> 00:02:44.709
Booked, ref R-2500.

47
00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:48.570
Caller sees CourierUnavailable: courier unreachable.

48
00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:52.283
Hold on to that third line, we'll come back to it.

49
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.544
Now let's break it on purpose.

50
00:02:54.544 --> 00:02:58.632
Delete the try and the catch, and keep the two lines inside.

51
00:02:58.632 --> 00:03:00.063
What happens in Java?

52
00:03:00.063 --> 00:03:01.017
And in Kotlin?

53
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:11.112
Java refuses to compile: unreported exception IOException; must be caught or declared to be thrown.

54
00:03:11.112 --> 00:03:16.907
The port's method doesn't declare IOException, so the compiler won't let it escape.

55
00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:19.365
Kotlin has no checked exceptions.

56
00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:26.533
So the same code compiles without a word, and prints: caller sees IOException: courier office closed: 503.

57
00:03:26.533 --> 00:03:30.657
The old client's exception walked straight through your port.

58
00:03:30.867 --> 00:03:33.426
For once, Java's fussiness is a feature.

59
00:03:33.426 --> 00:03:36.370
It gets in your way exactly where you need it.

60
00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:38.673
In Kotlin, that discipline is yours.

61
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.464
And that's where the travel plug picture breaks.

62
00:03:42.464 --> 00:03:44.493
A plug either fits, or it doesn't.

63
00:03:44.493 --> 00:03:47.357
A software adapter must also translate failures.

64
00:03:47.357 --> 00:03:50.699
A leaky one passes the foreign voltage straight through.

65
00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.134
So here's the rule.

66
00:03:52.134 --> 00:03:54.862
Translate at the edge, in both directions.

67
00:03:54.862 --> 00:03:56.680
Let nothing foreign through.

68
00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:58.936
Now that third line.

69
00:03:58.936 --> 00:04:04.281
When the port has only one method, as Courier does, you don't even need a class.

70
00:04:04.281 --> 00:04:09.559
A lambda, a small function written in place, is the adapter, in both languages.

71
00:04:09.559 --> 00:04:12.432
The demo prints: lambda adapter, QUICK-990.

72
00:04:13.333 --> 00:04:14.245
One guardrail.

73
00:04:14.245 --> 00:04:18.935
Keep the old library's names, formats and exceptions inside the adapter.

74
00:04:18.935 --> 00:04:24.797
If you find IOException, or the courier's XML, in any other class, the adapter has a leak.

75
00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:29.552
Switching couriers should mean writing one new adapter, and nothing else.

76
00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:32.546
So, why put anything in between?

77
00:04:32.546 --> 00:04:35.729
Because the in-between is where their words stop.

78
00:04:35.729 --> 00:04:38.263
The shop's code says: book this parcel.

79
00:04:38.263 --> 00:04:42.291
The adapter says it in XML, and turns their trouble into ours.

80
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:43.283
Quick check.

81
00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:47.718
A teammate's Kotlin adapter calls the old client with no try at all.

82
00:04:47.718 --> 00:04:49.544
The courier's office closes.

83
00:04:49.544 --> 00:04:51.892
What does the shop's order page see?

84
00:04:55.067 --> 00:04:57.748
IOException: courier office closed: 503.

85
00:04:57.748 --> 00:05:02.774
The courier's error leaked through the port, and the compiler said nothing.

86
00:05:02.774 --> 00:05:06.393
Catch it in the adapter, and throw CourierUnavailable.
